nt to  be equipped to answer them so to remind  you the problems with most of the prior  plans for how to pay for Medicare for  all  are mostly twofold problem one is that  many of the plans that say here's how  much it will cost and how we pay for it  they are assuming a drastic reduction in  health care costs in the u.s. to the  levels of Australia and Canada which are  about half of  hours in order to make the numbers work  but they don't say how they would do  that they say we will do it but they  don't say how and the reality is that  the health insurance bureaucracy that we  have does add cost to the system so if  you get rid of that with a Medicare for  All system you do shave some of the cost  but only a little bit of what you would  need to shave in order to cut the price  of our health care delivery by 50% which  is what Australia and Canada have if you  can't explain how to do that the numbers  don't work that's a problem with a lot  of the plans that are out there  number two the other issue is that a lot  of the plans for Medicare for all  include these phrases that nobody in the  middle class is going to pay more in the  net but we've not seen the middle class  defined and you can get pretty slippery  with that and then lastly there's also  the question of would Elizabeth Warren  or anyone else's plan make private  insurance illegal as an industry or  would it simply say let whatever happens  to private insurance happen if people  want it okay it'll probably go up and  cost as people get out of it and that's  it and I tend to favor that I think that  it's the I think it's legally difficult  to say this industry can no longer exist  anymore so I think the as they like to  say the free market solution should be  applied if hundreds of millions of  people get out of private insurance I  think the problem will sort of solve  itself okay  so let's look at Elizabeth Warren's plan  for how to pay for Medicare for all now  as we look at this list understand that  lots of this is controversial and I'm  not going to hide that from you first of  all Warren estimates the cost at twenty  point five trillion dollars in  additional spending by the federal  government over ten years this is much  lower than what the Urban Institute  analysis says they have a very detailed  projection which says it'll cost 34  trillion additional over 10 years that  alone like if that's true if the cost is  really thirty four instead of twenty and  a half that already makes Warren's math  completely wrong now there's already  some confusion about these numbers on  the one hand she says it'll cost twenty  point five trillion but on the other  hand she's  as on our website that it's a 52  trillion dollar over 10-year plan which  is it it's sort of both and this is  confusing and it relates to the fact  that some of the existing costs paid by  consumers would instead stay in the  pockets of consumers but then there will  be new spending that has to make up for  that which is what they call additional  federal spending but the idea is either  way the baseline is 52 trillion over ten  years in one case the government is in  the middle and that's what needs to be  additionally funded now if you look at  the savings that Elizabeth Warren lists  or where she would pay for these where  the money would come from to pay for  this additional spending  she says eight point eight trillion  comes from employers instead of paying  health insurance premiums to private  insurers they start paying the  government through a payroll tax that's  eight point eight trillion two trillion  comes from taxing the investment gains  of the top one percent one trillion  comes from a wealth tax on billionaires  800 billion comes from taxes on  financial transactions and then six  point one trillion is contributed by the  states that adds up and then she also  you know is assuming drastically lower  cost which we're going to talk about in  a moment these numbers add up if you  believe the additional spending is 20  and a half trillion instead of the 34  trillion which the Urban Institute says  it will and that gets us to the  assumptions about lower prescription  drug prices and reducing administrative  spending but more importantly reducing  what we pay for healthcare period and  this is where her plan falls short this  is not unique to Elizabeth Warren she  assumes that healthcare costs will  number one drop to be much closer to  those of other countries and number two  grow much more slowly than anticipated  you have to defend those assumptions  because they are huge complicated things  and as yet she doesn't appropriately  defend them in her plan this is not me  being a Warren attacker nor a warren  shill this is me just looking at the  data and the criticisms if you look at  Elizabeth Warren's plan  on her website it is assumed as a  foregone conclusion that health care  spending drops drastically and rapidly  and almost immediately when you switch  to Medicare for all I already told you  in my general and Aleks analysis of how  to pay for Medicare for all that serious  experts even experts on the Left say  that these expectations are unrealistic  there are estimates that in the best of  cases it could take decades to tamp down  health care costs in the u.s. to make  twenty point five trillion a realistic  number a more realistic number is 34  trillion as cited by the Urban Institute  so to be completely Frank this is a fine  plan it's not drastically different than  most of the other plans that we've seen  and it's it's a problem is it counts on  drastic reductions in health care  spending that cannot be assumed unless  you explain why is the Urban Institute  and other economists that have looked at  this why are they wrong and why is  Elizabeth Warren right or why is Bernie  right about the drastic reductions in  spending because you can't attribute it  simply to changing systems simply going  from a private insurance system to  Medicare for all doesn't drop health  care spending by the 50% it would need  to drop to match that of Australia or  Canada all this being said Medicare for  all may still be the best plan even at  34 trillion in additional spending over  ten years but we've got to figure out  how to pay for that it's doable but we  just have to be honest about the numbers  and this is now the trend that the  proposals that are being put forward for  how to pay for it have extraordinarily  optimistic numbers about the cost which  serious think tanks and economists  simply don't agree with that being said  Warren being more and more behind  Medicare for all that's good but we've  got to resolve this issue because  otherwise it either will not happen even  with a Democratic president where it'll  happen and we will be shocked at how  much more it will cost which is also a  problem if we're gonna do it let's do it  right based on what it'll actually cost  we've got to deal with it at some point  in time and I hope it gets dealt with  soon
 

